{
  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on comparing the probabilities of the two lotteries, particularly the probability of a 'favorable outcome.' A favorable outcome must be defined in context. In this decision problem, both lotteries involve losses or zero outcomes, so a 'favorable outcome' would reasonably be interpreted as avoiding a loss (i.e., getting 0 Euros) or minimizing the likelihood of losing money. Lottery A has a 75% chance of 0 Euros (thus a 25% chance of a loss). Lottery B has a 50% chance of 0 Euros and a 50% chance of a loss (25% for -4000 and 25% for -2000). Therefore, Lottery A has a higher probability (75%) of a non-loss outcome compared to Lottery B (50%). The decision reason would thus prefer Lottery A due to its higher probability of a favorable (non-loss) outcome. The relevant probability comparison is valid and can be derived from the lotteries.",
